speechtext:
May I conclude with this statement. Bearing in mind:
(1)	That the cost of rebuilding and equipping this great railway system is behind us and that in it we now have a railway capable of handling passenger and freight traffic as efficiently and economically as any of the great railways of the world;
(2)	That this system is o-perated iby men highly capable of performing their respective tasks and duties and who are loyal to the system to the last degree;
(3)	That the problem of motor transportation competition must of necessity be soon adjusted to the benefit of both the railways and the various means of motor transport ;
. (4) That vast benefits continue to accrue to Canada through the products of new wealth from the -farm, the forest, the mines and the great inland waters served by this railway system;
(5)	That tremendous sums of money are yearly saved to this dominion iby the comparatively low freight rates at which our freight is carried;
(6)	That we are now surely at the bottom of this period of depression and that better times are ahead-bearing these considerations in mind, I venture to say that the outlook is not so dark as it appears at the present and that the Canadian National railway system will not only weather this storm but, with a return to prosperity, we shall yet have in this great puiblicly-owned enterprise a transportation system which will pay its way and also return a -portion at least of the vast sums of money we have invested in it.
' Mr. OSCAR BOULANGER (Bellechasse) (Translation): Mr. Speaker, I would not be taking -part in this debate, had I not been invited to do so, a few days ago, by the hon. Postmaster General (Mr. Sauve). I regret that he is not in- his seat. He is probably busy dismissing the last Liberal postmaster left in this country. However, previous to -commenting on his speech, I wish to congratulate my hon. friend and neighbour, the member for Dorchester (Mr. Gagnon), who also is not in his seat, on the splendid speech he delivered this afternoon. I should have liked to congratulate him on- the progress he has made, for this last speech is quite an improvement over the one he delivered at the outset of the

I must apologize for
rising, but since the hon. member for all Vancouver directed two questions to me and would not allow me to answer I will take this opportunity of doing so. The hon. member asked me where I stood. He may not be able to understand this, but I have often stood for myself, and I have not always stood behind the various leaders of the Conservative party. Probably my hon. friend will not be able to understand that. On every fit occasion for the last thirty years I have always stated that there was no hurry to populate Canada with the- different European nations. Those who are here are all citizens of Canada and they are all our brothers, but throughout my whole life my attitude has been that this is the last of the new countries. During the thirty or thirty-five years I have considered this matter it has been my belief that Europe was becoming overcrowded, and the different countries were looking to Canada because, as I say, it was the last of the new countries. I always said there was no hurry to fill up our great west but that we should keep it for the children, and the children's children, of the two great pioneer races of Canada.
That always has been my position, and it will continue to be so. What is going to happen in the future if we fill up our great west? The two great pioneer races in Canada must, reserve a place for generations yet unborn.
What matter that at different times Our fathers claimed the sod;
What matter that at different shrines They prayed unto one God;
In fortune and in fame were bound In stronger links than steel;
And neither could be safe or sound But in the other's weal.
That has been my stand, I wish to inform the hon. member for all Vancouver, throughout my whole life.
